**Proof.** The change of coordinates $(\varepsilon,j)\leftrightarrow
(\varepsilon,k)$ is invertible, since $j=k-\bar\varepsilon$.
The value $\theta_m(\varepsilon,j)=(\varepsilon+h(k),k)$ determines
$k$, then $\varepsilon$, and hence $j$. Thus $\theta_m$ is injective.

The sum map, in the changed coordinates, is

\[
 (\varepsilon,k)\longmapsto
 \bigl(h(k),\,2k-\bar\varepsilon\bigr).
\]

Suppose two outputs agree. Reducing their second coordinates modulo
$2$ gives $\varepsilon=\varepsilon'$, because the terms $2k$ and
$2k'$ vanish there. The second coordinates then give $2k=2k'$ modulo
$2m$, hence $k=k'$ modulo $m$. The first coordinates give
$h(k)=h(k')$. In each of the two intervals $[0,m)$ and $[m,2m)$,
reduction modulo $m$ is injective, so the common half and residue
determine $k=k'$ modulo $2m$. The changed coordinates, and therefore
the original inputs, agree. Both maps are injective on the finite
group $H_m$, so both are bijective. $\square$

The graph of $\theta_m$ is a transversal of the row, column, and
sum-symbol projections on $H_m\times H_m$. Translating a transversal
by $(u,v)$ translates these three projected coordinates by $u$, $v$,
and $u+v$, respectively, so translation preserves the transversal
condition. The upper-half bit is the arithmetic datum that separates
the two lifts of a residue modulo $m$; without it, doubling loses
exactly that distinction. The theorem concerns the family $H_m$ and
does not assert a classification of complete mappings for all finite
groups.
